verifyReasoning:
The code confirms the main claim:
- sprint is bound to ControlLeft/ControlRight (input.js:33);
- _shouldBlockKey calls preventDefault (input.js:278-286), which Chromium ignores for reserved accelerators (Ctrl+W/T/N, Ctrl+Shift+W/T/N, Ctrl+Tab);
- keyboard lock is engaged only in fullscreen (menus.js:128-143);
- `grep -rn beforeunload src/ index.html` finds nothing;
- the only mitigations are the loading tip (menus.js:533) and the known-limitation note (docs/notes/player.md).

CDP can't trigger the browser accelerator itself, so I tested the close path with my script E:/playground/minecraft/tools/out/snap-w1/tools/out/vp/closetab.mjs. It runs a saved autostart world (save=1), makes edits, then calls Page.close (which runs beforeunload/pagehide like a user close) and reads IndexedDB from a new tab.
- Scenario A (code as is): no dialog, the tab closes immediately.
- Scenario B (the proposed handler injected): a 'beforeunload' dialog appears, so the user could cancel.
So the missing confirmation is real, and the proposed fix works.

The data-loss part is overstated. In scenario A, the async save from visibilitychange/pagehide persisted everything: 12/12 edited blocks and the new player position. Loss only appeared with many dirty chunks. With 169 modified chunks (A2), only 16/169 survived, the first SAVE_BATCH, because World._saveAll awaits each 16-chunk transaction before starting the next. With the handler and the dialog accepted (B2), 32/169 survived.

So the real impact is an abrupt exit to the desktop or browser whenever a Ctrl-sprinter presses W while Ctrl is held. That is frequent with the default bindings and disruptive, so major is defensible. Typical progress is not lost; only very large unsaved edits (e.g. /fill or explosions over more than 16 chunks) are.

suggestedFix:
Wrap the factory lookup in try/catch:
let idb = null; try { idb = options.indexedDB !== undefined ? options.indexedDB : globalThis.indexedDB ?? null; } catch (e) { warnFallback(e); }
Then fall back to MemoryBackend. Also add a .catch on the storage promise in main.js/Game.

refinedFix:
In Storage.open:
  let idb = null;
  try { idb = options.indexedDB !== undefined ? options.indexedDB : (globalThis.indexedDB ?? null); }
  catch (err) { warnFallback(err); return new Storage(new MemoryBackend()); }
Make the null check use options.indexedDB === null for the silent case.

Also harden Game: this._storageReady = Promise.resolve(storage).catch((err) => { console.warn('[game] storage unavailable:', err); return Storage.memory(); }).then((s) => (this.storage = s)).

Fix the repro note: the trigger is Firefox with cookies/site data blocked, or a third-party tracker context. Chromium sandboxed iframes are already handled.

Treat this as a SPEC §9.3 change. Implement Minecraft's lightmap in GLSL_LIGHT (tested in-page via tools/out/vrender/mclight.js):
  b = curve(blk) * 1.5;
  c = vec3(b, b*((b*0.6+0.4)*0.6+0.4), b*(b*b*0.6+0.4))
    + mix(vec3(daylight, daylight, 1.0), vec3(1.0), 0.35) * curve(sky) * (daylight*0.95+0.05);
  c = clamp(mix(c, vec3(0.75), 0.04), 0.0, 1.0);
  c = mix(c, 1.0 - pow(1.0 - c, vec3(4.0)), brightness);
  c = clamp(mix(c, vec3(0.75), 0.04), 0.0, 1.0);
Add brightness (default 0.5; Moody = 0, Bright = 1) to DEFAULT_SETTINGS and the options screen.

entityrenderer.js pastes GLSL_LIGHT in two programs (lines 680, 742) and calls lightColor() with its own uDaylight/uSkyTint uniforms. Brightness must reach those programs too, either as a uniform set by both renderers or as a constant baked into the source with programs rebuilt on change. Otherwise entities and the hand will no longer match the terrain.

Then drop NIGHT_SKY_TINT and update tests/renderer.test.js:43-45. If a full change is not accepted, adding the 1.5x block-light factor alone closes most of the torch and cave gap.
